[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-31597"] :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying  L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{e}^{x}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x]}^{′}}{[{e}^{x}]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x})}{{e}^{x}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2}{x{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-72339"] :question/worked-solution "Here the limits of the numerator and denominator are ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\infty $ and ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\infty ,$ respectively, so we have the case $\\frac{-\\infty }{\\infty }.$\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)]}^{′}}{[\\operatorname{csc}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}{-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\operatorname{cot}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\cancel{\\operatorname{cot}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡x} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-314152/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "e181f272-c259-5dfe-9f39-b3b9e5618d5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-314152/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "988ea093-d38d-51ab-8d06-7e5be72e5b6a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-314152/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer-field/choices ["q-314152/field-1/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-314152" :question/id #uuid "e190c4a3-1ee2-5dcd-b3fa-376f55f2ec5b" :question/math-academy-id "q-314152" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=0$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}]^{′}}{[{sin}^{2}⁡x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x{e}^{2x}-x+3{x}^{2}}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{[(1+2x){e}^{2x}-1+6x]^{′}}{[\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{(4+4x){e}^{2x}+6}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(4+0){e}^{0}+6}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+6}{2} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-314152/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-314152"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336897/field-1/56c57502ed9362f3f416b4398e79545a0252f6594946b2d60ff940ea8bef1e7b" :answer/id #uuid "80a63807-f383-573e-a43b-f82b10839bd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "44" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336897/field-1/2341d4beafc19cb7b8396e0b9f21c544ec7f6a786fc846e9361e36afd0b43437" :answer/id #uuid "440eb8e0-c5a3-524a-9bf3-4130be95649a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "43" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336897/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "74e7b4d9-3a93-54ee-942e-14e16a7bc102" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336897/field-1/56c57502ed9362f3f416b4398e79545a0252f6594946b2d60ff940ea8bef1e7b" :answer-field/choices ["q-336897/field-1/56c57502ed9362f3f416b4398e79545a0252f6594946b2d60ff940ea8bef1e7b" "q-336897/field-1/2341d4beafc19cb7b8396e0b9f21c544ec7f6a786fc846e9361e36afd0b43437"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336897" :question/id #uuid "4e31f629-06c9-5847-8488-ea8d7bd593df" :question/math-academy-id "q-336897" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\,{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128}{{x}^{2}-6x+9}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "If we plug in $x=3$ we get the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0},$ so  we apply L'Hopital's rule to find the limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128}{{x}^{2}-6x+9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128]}^{′}}{[{x}^{2}-6x+9]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{4x({x}^{2}-5)-48}{2x-6} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-10x-24}{x-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis time, the resulting limit still gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ To find the limit, we apply L'Hopital's rule once again. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{({x}^{2}-5)^{2}-48x+128}{{x}^{2}-6x+9} & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{2{x}^{3}-10x-24}{x-3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{{[2{x}^{3}-10x-24]}^{′}}{{[x-3]}^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 3}\\frac{6{x}^{2}-10}{1} \\\\  & =6(3)^{2}-10 \\\\  & =44.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-336897/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "8a41d750-ffd9-5226-9bd7-b763575870c1"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336897"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336951/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "d00c2043-386c-5a32-9492-22e6a626b056" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336951/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "445ecb86-4291-52cd-9f90-72e378f6a2ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336951/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer-field/choices ["q-336951/field-1/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336951" :question/id #uuid "e1391e51-b3fc-5d6d-af4b-284d914341dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-336951" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we have an indeterminate form of type $\\frac{\\infty }{\\infty },$ so applying L'Hopital's rule yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4}{3x+1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{[2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+9x-4]^{′}}{[3x+1]^{′}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{2}{x}+9)}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+9}{3} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-336951/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "aeebf57f-21dc-558a-bec5-4e97a5b26de4"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336951"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
